         PART F · HOW TO LISTEN
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-f-how-to-listen-building-an-active-ear">Part F · how to listen — building an active ear</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">Listening is a skill</p>
      <p class="cnote">Most people hear music passively — as emotional atmosphere or background. Active listening means attending to specific elements: following a melodic line through an ensemble, noticing when harmony changes and what effect the change has, identifying the structural moment (is this the exposition or the development?), hearing two independent contrapuntal voices simultaneously. This is not an innate talent — it is trained. Concert-goers in the 18th century routinely talked, ate, and moved around during performances; the convention of respectful silence is itself a historical construction, established in the 19th century with the rise of Romantic concert culture. Active listening transforms the experience of music from pleasant sensation into something more: a real-time encounter with another mind's organised thinking. It takes about 20–30 focused listening sessions with a single piece before its full structure becomes audible.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Listening guide multi-card -->
    <div class="mcard-grid" id="listenGrid"></div>
    <div id="listenDetail" class="mcard-detail"></div>

