        title: 'Sonata Form — the dominant structure of the Classical era',
        body: 'Three sections: Exposition (two contrasting themes, the second in a related key — creating harmonic departure), Development (themes broken apart, placed under harmonic stress — creating instability), and Recapitulation (both themes restated in the home key — resolving the tension). The genius of the form is that it generates drama purely through harmonic relationships: leaving home creates tension; returning home provides resolution. First movements of nearly every major Classical symphony, sonata, and string quartet use this structure. Beethoven\'s Fifth Symphony, first movement (6.5 min) is the clearest possible example.',
        tags: ['Classical era', 'Three-part structure', 'Harmonic drama', 'Beethoven · Mozart · Haydn']
