        title: 'Fugue — the highest form of contrapuntal writing',
        body: 'A fugue begins with a single theme (subject) stated alone. A second voice enters with the same theme (the answer, usually at the fifth) while the first voice continues independently (the countersubject). Further voices enter similarly until all are in motion. The central development uses the subject in various transformations: inversion (upside down), retrograde (backwards), augmentation (longer note values), diminution (shorter). Episodes between subject entries provide relief and harmonic motion. J.S. Bach\'s 48 preludes and fugues in the Well-Tempered Clavier remain the supreme examples; the Fugue in C# minor from Book I has five voices and runs to 87 bars of unbroken polyphony.',
        tags: ['Baroque', 'Counterpoint', 'Multiple voices', 'Bach · Handel']
      },
      'Theme and Variations': {
        title: 'Theme and Variations — identity through transformation',
        body: 'A theme (typically 16–32 bars) is stated, then repeated multiple times with systematic changes: new harmonisations, ornamented melody, changed rhythm, new texture, different mode (major to minor), changed tempo. The challenge is making each variation both recognisably related to the theme and distinct enough to sustain interest — while maintaining an overall shape from simple to complex, or from serious to playful. Beethoven\'s 32 Variations in C minor and Bach\'s Goldberg Variations (30 variations plus final reprise) are the form\'s landmarks. Brahms\'s Variations on a Theme by Handel (25 variations and a fugue) shows the Romantic extension of the form.',
        tags: ['All periods', 'Identity and transformation', 'Goldberg Variations', 'Brahms · Beethoven']
      },
