    const periods = {
      'Medieval': {
        title: 'Medieval (500–1400) — the foundations of Western notation',
        body: 'Gregorian chant established the first systematic notation (~9th century), enabling music to be transmitted accurately across time and geography — the invention that made Western classical music possible. Monophonic (single melodic line), in Latin, modal rather than tonal. The Notre Dame School (~1170–1250, Léonin and Pérotin) added a second voice against the chant, creating organum — the first polyphony. By 1300 (Ars Nova, Philippe de Vitry) rhythmic notation was sufficiently developed to write music of real rhythmic independence. Guillaume de Machaut (1300–1377) wrote the first complete polyphonic mass setting (Messe de Nostre Dame) — a structural model still in use.',
