        title: 'Renaissance (1400–1600) — polyphony perfected',
        body: 'The dominant form: sacred vocal polyphony (masses and motets for four to six voices). The key innovation was imitative counterpoint — voices entering successively with the same melodic fragment, creating a web of interrelated lines. Josquin des Prez (~1450–1521) was the first "great composer" in the modern sense — musicians across Europe knew and discussed his works by name. Giovanni Palestrina (1525–1594) codified the rules of Renaissance counterpoint so precisely that his style became the pedagogical gold standard — it is still taught in music schools as "species counterpoint." Secular music (madrigals, lute songs) developed alongside sacred music, with Monteverdi\'s madrigals pushing toward the emotional directness that would define the Baroque.',
        tags: ['Vocal polyphony', 'Josquin', 'Palestrina', 'Madrigal', 'Imitative counterpoint']
      },
