        title: 'Baroque (1600–1750) — the rise of tonality and opera',
        body: 'The period\'s two revolutions: opera (1600, Florence, Monteverdi\'s L\'Orfeo of 1607 is the earliest masterpiece still performed) and tonality (the systematic organisation of music around a home key). Tonality replaced the modal system and remained the harmonic foundation of Western music for the next 300 years. The orchestra coalesced as a standard ensemble. Bach synthesised every technique of the era in his final decade — the Well-Tempered Clavier (1722, 1744), the Mass in B minor, the Art of Fugue. Handel created the oratorio as a public concert form. Vivaldi established the three-movement concerto. The "continuous bass" (basso continuo) provided harmonic foundation for all ensemble music.',
        tags: ['Tonality', 'Opera', 'Bach', 'Handel', 'Vivaldi', 'Basso continuo']
      },
      'Classical': {
        title: 'Classical (1750–1820) — clarity, proportion, and formal architecture',
        body: 'A reaction against Baroque complexity: simplicity of texture (homophony replacing polyphony), clarity of phrase structure (regular 4- and 8-bar phrases), and formal balance. The symphony emerged as the dominant large form; the string quartet as the dominant chamber form; the piano sonata as the domestic form. Haydn (104 symphonies, 68 string quartets) invented the Classical style and the genre of the symphony as we know it. Mozart brought unparalleled melodic genius to every form. Beethoven spent his career straining against the limits of the Classical framework — his late works are the bridge between Classical and Romantic. The period represents Western music at its most rationally organised.',
        tags: ['Haydn', 'Mozart', 'Symphony', 'String quartet', 'Sonata form']
      },
      'Romantic': {
