        tags: ['Wagner', 'Brahms', 'Berlioz', 'Chopin', 'Nationalism', 'Expanded orchestra']
      },
      'Modern/Post-1900': {
        title: 'Modern / Post-1900 — the breaking of the system',
        body: 'Schoenberg\'s abandonment of tonality in 1908 ("free atonality") was the most radical break in the history of Western music — 300 years of tonal practice discarded in a single decade. His 12-tone method (serialism, 1923) created a new systematic organisation. Stravinsky\'s Rite of Spring (1913) showed that tonality could be retained but subjected to rhythmic violence that made the music feel new. After WWII, the Darmstadt school (Boulez, Stockhausen) systematised every musical parameter through total serialism. John Cage introduced chance procedures. Minimalism (Reich, Glass, Adams) rejected both complexity and atonality, returning to sustained harmony and repetitive patterns. Electronic music (from musique concrète in 1948) expanded the sonic universe beyond acoustic instruments entirely.',
