    const works = [
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Bach: Goldberg', sub:'Baroque', detail: { title:'J.S. Bach — Goldberg Variations (1741)', why:'A theme (aria) followed by 30 variations for harpsichord, covering every technique of keyboard writing: canons at every interval, a quodlibet (simultaneous popular tunes), virtuosic arabesque variations, and a final reprise of the aria. Glenn Gould\'s 1955 and 1981 recordings are defining interpretations. The 1981 recording, made shortly before Gould\'s death, is one of the great documents of recorded music.', time:'~50–80 min depending on repeats', why2:'Start here because it shows Bach\'s entire musical mind in a single work.' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Beethoven: 5th', sub:'Classical', detail: { title:'Beethoven — Symphony No. 5 in C minor (1808)', why:'The most famous opening in music history (G-G-G-Eb) is a rhythmic motif used as structural DNA throughout all four movements — the first time in history a symphony was unified across movements by a single idea. Unusually, all four movements connect without pause; the third movement\'s ominous scherzo leads directly into the triumphant finale. The finale\'s shift from C minor to C major is music\'s most architecturally satisfying gesture.', time:'~33 min', why2:'Start here because it demonstrates large-scale musical argument more clearly than any other work.' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Mozart: K. 626', sub:'Classical', detail: { title:'Mozart — Requiem in D minor (1791)', why:'Left unfinished at Mozart\'s death and completed by his pupil Süssmayr. The opening Introit (Requiem aeternam) and Kyrie are entirely Mozart\'s; the extent of his contribution to subsequent movements is debated by scholars. The work combines the grandeur of Baroque counterpoint with Classical melodic grace. The Lacrimosa (8 bars — all Mozart wrote) is among the most directly affecting music in the repertoire.', time:'~55 min', why2:'Start here for the intersection of Mozart\'s voice with Baroque tradition.' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Wagner: Tristan', sub:'Romantic', detail: { title:'Wagner — Tristan und Isolde (1865)', why:'The "Tristan chord" (F-B-D#-G#) in the first bar of the Prelude is the most analysed chord in music history. Its unresolved ambiguity — it seems to belong to several keys simultaneously — opened the door to harmonic impressionism, atonality, and 20th-century harmonic practice. The opera itself (4+ hours) is built on a small number of leitmotifs developed with hallucinatory intensity. Debussy, Schoenberg, and every subsequent composer absorbed its harmonic language.', time:'~4 hours (opera)', why2:'Start with the Prelude and Liebestod (love-death, ~12+7 min) if the full opera is too daunting.' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Debussy: La Mer', sub:'Impressionist', detail: { title:'Debussy — La Mer (1905)', why:'Three orchestral movements depicting the sea: "From dawn to noon on the sea," "Play of the waves," "Dialogue of wind and sea." The work avoids depicting the sea literally (no programme notes, no mimicry) and instead creates an atmosphere — shifting harmonies, layered woodwind colour, polyrhythmic waves. The brass writing in the finale is among the most original in the orchestral repertoire. Debussy\'s use of whole-tone harmonics and parallel movement made this the sonic manifesto of musical Impressionism.', time:'~25 min', why2:'Start here for the most accessible introduction to non-German late Romantic orchestration.' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Stravinsky: Rite', sub:'20th c.', detail: { title:'Stravinsky — The Rite of Spring (1913)', why:'Stravinsky described it as "a purely musical work." Its harmonic language is bitonal (two keys simultaneously), its rhythmic patterns asymmetrical and irregular (7/4, 11/4, 5/4 in rapid succession), and its orchestration brutal: the famous opening bassoon solo begins in the very top of the instrument\'s range — a deliberately harsh, cramped sound. The work established that "ugliness" was available as an aesthetic resource. It caused a riot at its premiere; it is now among the most frequently performed orchestral works.', time:'~35 min', why2:'Start here to understand the moment Western music broke with its past.' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Schubert: Winterreise', sub:'Romantic', detail: { title:'Schubert — Winterreise (1828)', why:'A song cycle of 24 settings of Wilhelm Müller\'s poems — a journey through winter landscape as an extended metaphor for despair and alienation. Written in the last year of Schubert\'s life (he died at 31), it is among the most emotionally extreme works in the vocal repertoire. The harmonic language is unprecedented: modulations to unexpected keys, unresolved dissonances, deliberate tonal ambiguity. The final song ("Der Leiermann" — The Hurdy-Gurdy Man) is a drone over which a wanderer with nothing to lose contemplates following a street musician into the void.', time:'~70 min', why2:'Start here to understand the Romantic Lied (art song) at its most extreme.' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Bach: St Matthew', sub:'Baroque', detail: { title:'J.S. Bach — St Matthew Passion (1727)', why:'Setting of the Gospel of Matthew\'s account of the Passion, for two orchestras, two choirs, and soloists. Running nearly 3 hours, it is among the largest choral works in the repertoire and the culmination of the German Passion tradition. The aria "Erbarme dich" (Have mercy, Lord) for alto solo and solo violin is one of the most overwhelming moments in all music. Felix Mendelssohn\'s 1829 revival of the Passion — 79 years after Bach\'s death — began the Bach revival and established the historical performance movement.', time:'~3 hours', why2:'Start here to understand the Baroque sacred choral tradition at its most ambitious.' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Beethoven: Op.131', sub:'Late Classical', detail: { title:'Beethoven — String Quartet No. 14 in C# minor, Op. 131 (1826)', why:'Seven movements played without pause, beginning with a slow fugue and ending with a violent presto. Composed when Beethoven was completely deaf, it sounds nothing like any music that came before it. The harmonic language, the rhythmic irregularity, the treatment of silence as a structural element — all anticipate Bartók, Schoenberg, and the music of 100 years later. Schubert reportedly wept after hearing it. Franz Schubert said of it: "After this, what is there left for us to write?"', time:'~40 min', why2:'Start here to understand the outer limits of tonal music before atonality.' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Mahler: 9th', sub:'Late Romantic', detail: { title:'Mahler — Symphony No. 9 (1909, prem. 1912)', why:'Mahler\'s last completed symphony, written under the shadow of the death of his daughter and his own cardiac diagnosis. The finale Adagio is the most sustained leave-taking in the symphonic repertoire — 25 minutes of increasing fragmentation and silence, ending with barely audible strings that simply stop. Leonard Bernstein famously said it was "the greatest symphony ever written." Its harmonic language is at the border of atonality; the first movement uses bitonality decades before it became commonplace.', time:'~80 min', why2:'Start here to understand where the Romantic tradition went in its final moment of self-consciousness.' } }
    ];

