        title: 'Hindustani and Carnatic — the world\'s most elaborate improvisational systems',
        body: 'Indian classical music is split into two main streams: Hindustani (North India, influenced by Mughal court music) and Carnatic (South India, more strictly governed by composed pieces). Both are built around the raga (a melodic framework specifying which notes to use, how to ornament them, and what emotional quality to evoke) and the tala (a rhythmic cycle). A Hindustani raga performance typically begins with an alap — a slow, unmeasured improvisation establishing the raga\'s personality — followed by jor (medium speed), then jhala (fast), and finally a composed piece (gat or bandish) against a rhythmic cycle maintained by tabla or pakhawaj. The most revered performers — Ravi Shankar, Bismillah Khan, Bhimsen Joshi — were considered national treasures in India. The tradition has no equivalent concept of a "composer" in the Western sense: the raga system itself is compositional, and every performance is simultaneously composition and interpretation.',
