        title: 'West African drumming — the mother tongue of global popular music',
        body: 'West African (particularly Yoruba, Akan, and Mande) musical traditions are characterised by polyrhythm: multiple simultaneous rhythmic patterns in different metres that together create a dense rhythmic texture no single player could reproduce. The clave pattern (used in son, salsa, and Afro-Cuban music) is a West African structural device. The djembe, talking drum (dundun), and balafon (wooden xylophone) are principal instruments. Crucially, music in these traditions is inseparable from movement (dance), ceremony, and community function — the Western concert hall separation of performance from participation is alien to the tradition. This tradition, transported to the Americas through the slave trade, produced the blues, jazz, gospel, funk, soul, and hip-hop. The entire global popular music ecosystem traces directly to this source.',
