    const genreCards = [
      { icon:'fa-guitar', label:'Delta Blues', sub:'1900–1940', detail: { title:'Delta Blues — the ur-form', body:'Emerged in the Mississippi Delta around 1900, codified by W.C. Handy\'s 1912 "Memphis Blues" (the first published blues). The 12-bar form (I-I-I-I-IV-IV-I-I-V-IV-I-I in Roman numerals) and the call-and-response structure are the defining features. Robert Johnson (1911–1938) recorded 29 songs in 1936–37 that established the Delta Blues canon. The myth of Johnson selling his soul at the crossroads was likely invented by his contemporaries — but it reveals how his playing seemed to transcend normal human technique.', ex:'Robert Johnson, Charley Patton, Son House' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'New Orleans Jazz', sub:'1895–1930', detail: { title:'New Orleans Jazz — the birth of improvisation', body:'Developed in Storyville (the New Orleans red-light district) from roughly 1895. Key innovation: collective improvisation — cornet, clarinet, and trombone improvising simultaneously on the same harmonic framework. Louis Armstrong\'s Hot Five and Hot Seven recordings (1925–1928) transformed jazz from a collective ensemble music into a soloist\'s art. Armstrong\'s technical facility and melodic invention were so far beyond his contemporaries that he effectively created the vocabulary of jazz improvisation.', ex:'Louis Armstrong, Jelly Roll Morton, King Oliver' } },
      { icon:'fa-music', label:'Bebop', sub:'1945–1960', detail: { title:'Bebop — virtuosity as artistic statement', body:'Charlie Parker ("Bird") and Dizzy Gillespie developed bebop in after-hours jam sessions at Harlem clubs (Monroe\'s Uptown House, Minton\'s Playhouse) from about 1942. Deliberately complex — fast tempos (200+ BPM), extended chord substitutions, virtuosic unison heads — to exclude musicians who couldn\'t keep up. Parker\'s alto saxophone lines at 300+ notes per minute over complex chord changes remain technically formidable 80 years later. Thelonious Monk\'s idiosyncratic piano style, with deliberate "wrong" notes that were actually harmonic extensions, created a new approach to jazz piano.', ex:'Charlie Parker, Dizzy Gillespie, Thelonious Monk, Bud Powell' } },
      { icon:'fa-drum', label:'Soul / R&B', sub:'1954–present', detail: { title:'Soul and R&B — gospel fire and secular words', body:'Soul music is essentially gospel music (call-and-response, testifying, melismatic vocal runs) applied to secular subjects (love, pain, joy). Ray Charles was the first to make this explicit, combining gospel piano with blues lyrics in 1954. Aretha Franklin\'s recordings for Atlantic (1967–1972) are the genre\'s summit: "Respect" (1967), "Think" (1968), "Chain of Fools" (1968). James Brown\'s funk (beginning with "Papa\'s Got a Brand New Bag," 1965) stripped the music down to rhythm — "the one" (the first beat of the bar) became the entire grammar.', ex:'Ray Charles, Aretha Franklin, James Brown, Otis Redding' } },
      { icon:'fa-record-vinyl', label:'Rock & Roll', sub:'1951–present', detail: { title:'Rock and Roll — the global genre', body:'The fusion of blues (harmonic structure, emotional directness), gospel (rhythm, intensity), and country (instrumentation, narrative lyrics) that emerged from Memphis and Cleveland around 1951. Chuck Berry and Little Richard established the form; Elvis Presley made it white and commercially dominant. The Beatles\' 1964 US invasion was a return of Americanised African-American music to its source, filtered through British sensibility. By Revolver (1966) and Sgt. Pepper (1967) the Beatles were making studio recordings that could not be performed live — and effectively invented the concept album.', ex:'Chuck Berry, Elvis Presley, The Beatles, The Rolling Stones' } },
      { icon:'fa-microphone', label:'Hip-Hop', sub:'1973–present', detail: { title:'Hip-Hop — the last great American art form', body:'Originated at a block party in the South Bronx on August 11, 1973, when DJ Kool Herc isolated and extended the "break" (the percussion section) of funk records by switching between two copies of the same record. From that technical innovation grew an entire culture: DJing (manipulation of recorded music), MCing (vocal improvisation over beats), breakdancing, and graffiti art. Grandmaster Flash, Run-D.M.C., and Public Enemy established the form. Kendrick Lamar\'s To Pimp a Butterfly (2015), using jazz musicians playing live in the studio, demonstrated that hip-hop could achieve the formal and musical complexity of any other serious art form.', ex:'DJ Kool Herc, Public Enemy, Jay-Z, Kendrick Lamar' } }
    ];

